    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d886">

                <pb facs="0761" n="749" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg749"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.51 Public Works Debt/298</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Messersmith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 84</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-03-28">March 28, 1940</date>.<lb/>[Received April
                        1.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to my despatch
                    No. 49 of March 19, 1940, with which I transmitted memoranda covering
                    conversations with the Secretary of State, Dr. Campa, and with the President,
                    Dr. Laredo Brú. In view of further information which came into my possession, I
                    deemed it advisable to seek another conversation with the President, and was
                    able to do so on March 25, 1940. There is transmitted herewith a memorandum of
                    my conversation<anchor n="29" xml:id="d886-f1"/> with him which <pb facs="0762"
                        n="750" xml:id="pg750"/> is self-explanatory and which I believe the
                    Department will find of interest.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George S.
                    Messersmith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d886-f1" n="29" xml:id="d886-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. Ambassador Messersmith informed the Cuban President and Secretary of
                    State of reported activities by certain persons to delay action in an effort to
                    get some personal advantage from the settlement of the public works debt.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
